# dependencies
node_modules/
.bun-install-cache/
# bun resolves from the workspace root, so a lockfile inside a package is a stale second
# source of truth. One was committed at packages/mizan-core/bun.lock and desynchronised
# from the root; `git rm`'d. If one reappears, it is a bug, not a deliverable.
packages/*/bun.lock

# secrets — nothing under these patterns may ever be committed (gate G-4 / gitleaks)
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
#
# There is deliberately NO `!.github/*.pem` exception. One was here, and it silently undid the
# two rules above it for the entire directory a judge or an agent is most likely to add a file to:
#
#   git check-ignore --no-index -v .github/evil.pem
#   .gitignore:15:!.github/*.pem
#
# A private key committed under `.github/` is still a committed private key, and it is worse than
# one committed elsewhere, because that is where a workflow file or a CI helper gets added. The
# negated pattern is also invisible: it reads as a special case with a reason, and the reason is
# in the diff history rather than in the file. So the exception is gone, and the two commands
# that prove the remaining patterns work are recorded here instead.

# corpus snapshot: large and reproducible from pinned URLs + attestation.json
data/corpus.db
data/corpus.db-shm
data/corpus.db-wal
# SQLite's rollback journal, written whenever the DB is opened outside WAL mode.
# Same ~80 MB as the DB itself, and just as reproducible — so it is never the artefact.
data/corpus.db-journal
data/raw/
# generated per-row registry (large; the committed sources.json is the deliverable)
data/registry/records.jsonl

# provenance ledgers: `data/ledger.jsonl` (corpus ingest) and `data/runs.jsonl` (runs) are
# append-only and are committed as evidence.
#
# There were `data/ledger/*.tmp` and `data/ledger/*.bak` rules here and they were fiction:
# `data/ledger/` has never existed — the ledgers are single files directly under `data/` — and
# nothing in this codebase writes a `.tmp` or `.bak` sibling beside either one. An append-only
# writer has no temp-file-and-rename step, so there is no transient artefact to ignore, and the
# SQLite sidecars that genuinely are transient are listed above with the database.
#
# Recorded rather than silently deleted, because the pattern here is the actual defect: a rule
# that names nothing real reads as coverage. It is not coverage. It is a claim, and the same way
# of writing put six false file paths in DISCLOSURE.md — which is what `bun run check:docs` now
# fails the build over.

# ============================================================
# PUBLISHED — apps/, data/ and scripts/ are tracked.
# ============================================================
# This block previously withheld apps/, data/ and scripts/ "pending review". That block was
# removed AND the three directories were committed, because a repository that cannot be cloned
# and built is not a deliverable:
#
#   - `apps/cli` is the Story 11 kill-gate deliverable.
#   - `data/registry/sources.json` is a MANDATED challenge deliverable (the
#     source/tool/licence registry) and gate G-5 audits it.
#   - `scripts/` holds the CI, ingest and ledger drivers ci.yml invokes.
#
# Removing the blanket `data/` rule does NOT un-ignore the large artefacts: the specific rules
# above still exclude data/corpus.db (~80 MB), data/registry/records.jsonl (~16 MB) and
# data/raw/. They are reproducible from the pinned URLs in attestation.json, so the evidence
# about them is committed while the bytes are not.
#
# ## The two assertions, and why they are different commands
#
# "Tracked" and "not ignored" are independent properties, and each has exactly one command
# that proves it. Conflating them is how this file came to claim a commit that had not
# happened, so they are named separately:
#
#   - NOT IGNORED  `git check-ignore --no-index <path>`  (or `git check-ignore` without the flag)
#     Exits 0 and prints the path only when git WOULD ignore it. A silent, non-zero exit is
#     the pass. This proves nothing about whether the bytes are committed.
#   - TRACKED      `git ls-files -- <path>`
#     Prints one line per tracked path. An empty result means the file exists on disk and is
#     NOT in the repository — the exact state that made a clean clone unbuildable.
#
# The regression this block caused, and which `git ls-files -- apps data scripts` exposes as
# zero lines: `bun install --frozen-lockfile` failed with "lockfile had changes, but lockfile
# is frozen" because bun.lock declares @mizan/cli@workspace:apps/cli and withholding apps/
# desynchronised the lockfile. `ingest:check` and `verify:ledger` then failed on missing
# modules. Nothing after step 1 was reached. Verified end to end on a clean clone.

# test + build output
coverage/
dist/
out/
*.tsbuildinfo
*.log

# python. `submission/make_deck.py` is a tracked SOURCE file and stays tracked; what is ignored is
# CPython's own build output. `make_deck.cpython-314.pyc` was tracked until now, swept in by
# Story 1's blanket "commit the entire working tree" rather than by any story: it is a
# CPython-version-specific binary, unreviewable in a diff, and every interpreter that runs the
# deck generates a different one. `docs-surfaces.ts` already special-cased `__pycache__` when walking
# documents, so the tool knew the directory was noise while git was shipping it.
# Both patterns are needed: the directory rule covers `__pycache__/`, the extension rule covers a
# `.pyc` written anywhere else (which is where a `py_compile` run puts one).
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# OS cruft. desktop.ini and ehthumbs.db are here because CI runs
# windows-latest and a Windows clone would otherwise commit them.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
ehthumbs.db

# editors. The whole directory is ignored, but these are the files
# that escape when the directory is not in a fresh clone.
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
*.orig
*.rej

# agent/editor local state written into the project directory
.opencode/

# requirement/planning notes written by the office workflow, not deliverables of this repository.
#
# `packages/mizan-gate/src/docs-gates.ts` already declares `specs/` in GATE_CLAIM_EXCLUDES with the
# reason: it records what was ASKED FOR at a point in time, so a gate-count claim inside it is a
# historical note and not a claim about what this repository runs. That declaration and this rule are
# the same decision stated twice, deliberately (AGENTS.md 17): the gate must not fail the build over
# planning prose, and git must not ship planning prose as if it were the design record.
#
# The workflow writes two revisions of one note under both `specs/` and `specs/adr/`, so committing
# the directory would also commit two divergent declarations of a single decision — the hazard
# section 17 exists to prevent. The design records that ARE deliverables live in
# `docs/specs/adr/`, which is tracked and which `docs-adr.ts` audits.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash, and that is load-bearing rather than tidiness. An unanchored
# `specs/` also matches `docs/specs/`, so the first version of this rule silently un-tracked ADR-19
# through ADR-23 while `git status` went quiet — four deliverables on disk and absent from the
# repository, which is precisely the "a rule that names nothing real reads as coverage" failure this
# file already records about its own earlier entries. The two commands that settle it:
#   git check-ignore --no-index -v docs/specs/adr/ADR-19.md   # must print nothing (exits 1)
#   git check-ignore --no-index -v specs/adr/<note>.md        # must print the rule (exits 0)
/specs/
